Using a WebSocket combined with your AI agent can give you real-time, efficient communication, but to avoid unnecessary AI calls, you'll still need some smart caching and change detection on the UI side.


---

✅ When & Why to Use WebSocket

Using WebSocket makes sense if:

You want to push AI summary results as soon as they’re ready.

You want to notify the backend that a page has changed, and only then request a new summary.

You expect high-frequency updates and want low-latency comms between frontend and AI agent.


But just using WebSocket alone won’t reduce AI calls — you still need logic to decide when to trigger AI, which leads us to the key strategy:


---

✅ Strategy: Use WebSocket + Content Hashing for Intelligent AI Calls

1. Client-side Hashing for Change Detection

Before sending a request via WebSocket, compute a hash of the page content.

Send this hash to the backend.

Backend stores the last summary per hash (cache).


2. Backend Receives Hash and Looks Up Cache

If the hash is already summarized → return cached summary.

If it’s a new hash → call AI agent, store hash → summary mapping in Redis/DB, and send back result via WebSocket.



---

✅ Flow Diagram

[UI: User Clicks Summarize]
        ↓
[Generate content hash]
        ↓
[WebSocket: send hash + optional text]
        ↓
[Backend]
    ├─> If hash exists → return cached summary via WebSocket
    └─> Else → Call AI agent → store summary → send back


---

✅ Sample WebSocket Payload

{
  "type": "summary_request",
  "hash": "abc123",
  "content": "optional, only if new"
}


